Closes the post-first-frame fault-loop class (C000001D, Mach UNHANDLED,
JIT-pool host-PC leakage) by emitting a 3-instruction bounds check
before every callret stack push/pop in the ARM64EC JIT. Required
because iOS Wine's VirtualAlloc(PAGE_NOACCESS) doesn't enforce the
guard pages around the callret allocation, so the SEH-driven
HandleAccessViolation reset never fires on underflow — the stack
drifts silently into "guard" memory until JIT code corrupts itself.

Architecture:
- New CPUState::callret_sp_base (repurposes _pad1, same offset/size),
  populated from Thread->CallRetStackBase in CallRetStack::InitializeThread.
- Inline guard at 4 emit sites:
    BranchOps.cpp ~146 — linked-path CALL push
    BranchOps.cpp ~192 — RET pop
    BranchOps.cpp ~245 — Skip-path CALL push (after L1 cache miss)
    Dispatcher.cpp ~549 — JITCallback sentinel push
  Each emits: ldr base from State; sub sp-base; lsr #24; cbz skip;
  ldr base again; add #0x400000 (= SIZE/4 DefaultLocation). Steady-state
  overhead is 3 ARM64 instructions per CALL/RET emit.

Diagnostics (log-only, no behavior change):
- JITPOOL_RIP detector in Core.cpp CompileBlock when GuestRIP is in
  the JIT RX pool — tells us when host PCs leak into State.rip.
- CALLRET_OOB reset-on-compile-entry as a catch-net for the rare case
  where the JIT-side guard doesn't catch it.
- CB_SUMMARY periodic counter (every 16K invocations) separating
  total invocations from real cache misses. Settled the 180K-compile
  hot RIP question: 99% are cache hits, real_compiles is ~7K total.

Log-volume reductions:
- Suppressed per-CompileBlock RIP log (was 6× amplified by Wine's
  file trace, dwarfing real signal).
- Suppressed per-IR-op Arm64JIT dispatch logs.
- Suppressed all Frontend Decoder per-block logs.
Result: ~5-10× smaller log files at no visibility cost; iOS log
capacity now covers ~10 min of Thumper runtime cleanly.

Verified result on Thumper:
- Splash (Present #1) rendered consistently
- 360+ Presents over 6+ seconds before next wall
- Zero C000001D, zero C0000005, zero NtTerminateProcess
- "Splash → black" transition matches Windows reference behavior
- New wall is an FMOD worker NULL-deref loop at fmod64+0x7c400,
  unrelated to dispatcher health

